                        "entry_id": "1db637d0-f09f-3ad6-ac3a-ccf4a54da8b3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Symphonie de diverses voix ou instruments, à l’octave ou double octave."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f8addcf8-3908-4374-8865-69256a635580",
                                "sense_id": "a7d35654-8cfd-3ac2-8ae6-305e69626ac0",
                                "text": "Les voix et les instruments peuvent concerter ensemble, à sçavoir : à l’unisson, ce que les Grecs appelloient homophonie ; ou à l’octave, & quelquefois à la double octave, ce qu’ils appelloient antiphonie."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bd4edd4d-c5eb-3de1-840f-7ebcb2e1281b",
                        "entry_id": "1db637d0-f09f-3ad6-ac3a-ccf4a54da8b3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Chant alterné, responsorial."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b7f64bed-b8dd-4a54-9607-00d9577e3234",
                        "source_entry_id": "1db637d0-f09f-3ad6-ac3a-ccf4a54da8b3",
                        "target_lang_word": "antiphony",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "antiphony",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7ab136d9-ae3d-3189-bab9-7e0d8efae3e3",
                                        "entry_id": "af1e841d-d3cd-3772-9e9c-9ba2cf843e86",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternate, or responsive singing by a choir split into two parts; a piece sung or chanted in this manner."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6aeff02c-bab0-3d2f-b603-7d7401115d21",
                                        "entry_id": "af1e841d-d3cd-3772-9e9c-9ba2cf843e86",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternate, or responsive ideas or opinions; juxtaposition."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "04c59e59-816d-49e6-8453-5aa5240931af",
                                                "sense_id": "6aeff02c-bab0-3d2f-b603-7d7401115d21",
                                                "text": "Besides that Cardan pleases himself with that Antiphonie in Nature, that as the Ostrich being a Bird, yet never shes in the Aire, so this Bird of Paradise should alwayes be in the Aire, and never rest upon the Earth."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3cf7683c-c5cb-3c69-9321-c1805f03bbc5",
                                        "entry_id": "af1e841d-d3cd-3772-9e9c-9ba2cf843e86",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of apophony (“contrastive vowel modification”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0ab79668-48e0-4089-86a9-c09fefc78c6b",
                                                "sense_id": "3cf7683c-c5cb-3c69-9321-c1805f03bbc5",
                                                "text": "Still another phase of language which interests Professor Orr is that of vowel antiphony in the functional development of language; e.g. the “front-back” vowel sequence (tittle-tattle, et patati et patata, etc.)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1ba97414-ebf3-424f-baaa-69e14c01d48f",
                                                "sense_id": "3cf7683c-c5cb-3c69-9321-c1805f03bbc5",
                                                "text": "Roman Jakobson gives evidence to show how language uses ‘antiphony’ or vowel opposition in pairs of words with opposite meanings: ‘gleam’ and ‘gloom’, ‘here’ and ‘there’, or in French ‘petit’ and ‘grand’."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "557e8865-9c46-4cbf-899c-4d9dcffca8a2",
                                                "sense_id": "3cf7683c-c5cb-3c69-9321-c1805f03bbc5",
                                                "text": "The various types of reduplication […] include full reduplication (tum-tum), […] vowel antiphony (flip-flop, criss-cross) […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
